# What never reaches the build context. Everything the image needs is a source
# file or a lockfile; everything below is output, and output that travels from
# a laptop into an image is output nothing reproduced.

.git

# Reinstalled by `npm ci` in the frontend stage, against the committed lockfile.
# It is also the largest directory in this repository by a wide margin.
**/node_modules

# Rebuilt by `dotnet publish` in the backend stage. On a laptop these hold
# binaries for the developer's own architecture, which is not necessarily the
# one being built for.
**/bin
**/obj

# A developer's local database, its write-ahead log and its shared-memory index.
# The image ships no data; the volume it declares is where data goes.
**/*.db
**/*.db-wal
**/*.db-shm

# The SPA's build output, mirroring .gitignore: everything in wwwroot except the
# vendored stylesheet, which is a committed source file rather than a build's.
#
# This is not tidiness. `npm run build` on a laptop leaves hashed bundles in
# assets/, their names are unique per build, and nothing in the image build
# overwrites or deletes them — so a stale one would be copied in and served
# alongside the bundles this image actually built. The image builds the SPA
# itself, from source, every time.
backend/src/Kairos.Api/wwwroot/*
!backend/src/Kairos.Api/wwwroot/kairos.css

# Nothing below is an input to the image, and every one of them is edited often.
# That is the point: the backend stage does `COPY backend/ ./`, so a file inside
# that tree which the publish never reads still invalidates the layer and buys a
# full rebuild. backend/tests/ is the expensive one — the API project is what is
# published, and the test project is not restored, built or referenced by it.
docs
.github
scripts
backend/tests

# Never in the context at all. Nothing here reads one, and an image is a bad
# place to find out that something did.
**/.env
**/.env.*
